@* User Guide — Project & evidence: the logical Project model, Source Analysis, Explorers, generated documentation and traceability. *@
@using BirkNext.Web.Services
@using static BirkNext.Web.Components.Guide.GuideCallout

<section id="project-model" class="ug-section ug-topic" data-topic="project-model" aria-labelledby="project-model-title">
    <h2 id="project-model-title" class="ug-section-title" tabindex="-1">Project model and binding</h2>
    <GuideArticleMeta Topic="project-model" />
    <p class="ug-lead"><strong>Target Application is the system under test; Project is its evidence.</strong> The <a href="@UserGuideContent.TargetEnvironmentTab("target")">Target Application tab</a> says WHAT an environment represents (for example M2LB — chosen from detected applications or added manually). The <a href="@UserGuideContent.TargetEnvironmentTab("project")">Project tab</a> then binds the imported packages that describe it: application source, documentation, contracts, infrastructure and supporting packages. Packages remain independent evidence providers with their own history.</p>
    <p data-testid="guide-project-scopes"><strong><a href="/project-scopes">Systems &amp; Scopes</a></strong> shows the systems and services BirkNext found in the imported source for the active Target Environment, how they connect, and the project's shared scopes. The page never starts an analysis by itself: when nothing is shown it says why (no Target Environment, no source imported, source not analysed yet, an analysis still running, or an analysis that found no systems). While an analysis runs, the previous result stays visible with a note and a Refresh button and links to the place that fixes it. Technical ids are under <em>Technical details</em>.</p>
    <p data-testid="guide-project-scopes-systems"><em>Systems</em>: search by name or any other name, filter by kind (system or service), by whether a decision is needed, and by scope membership. A service is shown under a system only when that system declares the service's name as one of its own names; a similar name is never enough. Open a row for its details: where the identity comes from, other names, services, connections, the configured location in the Target Environment (configuration only, not a verified connection) and its scopes. Identity questions (another name for a system, two entries that might be the same system) can be answered there; the answer is recorded in the shared review history used by Relationship Review.</p>
    <p data-testid="guide-project-scopes-connections"><em>Connections</em> lists every connection in the project first; pick a system to see only its incoming and outgoing connections. Each connection shows what states it and how far it is established: <em>from source evidence</em>, <em>confirmed by a person</em>, <em>candidate</em> or <em>needs review</em>, <em>rejected</em> (hidden unless you filter for it), and <em>observed at runtime</em> only when runtime evidence saw it. Connections come from the source analysis (including two services that produce and consume the same message channel) and from Integration Topology. When a system has no connection, the page says that no evidence states one: BirkNext does not add a connection from expectations or similar names. Review links open Relationship Review, with a link back.</p>
    <p data-testid="guide-project-scopes-scopes"><em>Scopes</em> are reusable, versioned selections of what you test together. <em>Create scope</em> walks through three steps: details (name, purpose, description), members (pick systems, scenarios, requirements, user stories, features, journeys, contracts and Test Data from searchable catalogs; any kind is optional, so a scope of only scenarios or requirements is fine), and review. Saving creates a draft; an empty draft can be saved but not approved. An id typed under <em>Advanced</em> is checked against its catalog first, and ids the catalog does not confirm are refused. <em>Submit for review</em> marks a draft as ready; <em>Approve</em> pins that revision (recorded with who and why). Editing an approved scope creates a new draft revision and the approved one never changes; <em>Compare</em> shows what was added or removed between two revisions. A member that later disappears from its catalog stays in the scope and is marked for review. Approval does not mean tests passed and never authorizes running tests.</p>
    <p data-testid="guide-project-scopes-consumers">In <a href="/test-data">Test Data</a> and <a href="/test-plan-suggestions">Test Plan Suggestions</a>, <em>Filter by scope</em> narrows the list to what one or several approved scopes name (shared members are counted once). Test Data shows the scope's assets and how many scope members are missing from the catalog; Test Plan Suggestions shows the scope's scenarios plus the scenarios that verify the scope's requirements through an established link (stated by the source or confirmed by a person; candidates are never used). <a href="/integration-quality-review">Integration Journeys</a> shows the journeys the scope names. Without a scope selected these pages work as before.</p>
    <div class="ug-compare">
        <div class="ug-compare-col ug-compare-yes"><p class="ug-compare-label">Project is</p><ul><li>One logical system, such as an application and its contracts</li><li>A set of evidence roles, each bound to packages</li><li>References only — imports and snapshots keep their owners</li></ul></div>
        <div class="ug-compare-col ug-compare-no"><p class="ug-compare-label">Project is not</p><ul><li>The newest import</li><li>One ZIP that must contain everything</li><li>A copy of the evidence</li></ul></div>
    </div>

    <h3 id="package-types" class="ug-subtitle">Valid package types</h3>
    <p>Each of these is valid project evidence. Missing code never makes a package invalid.</p>
    <div class="ug-chip-row" role="list">
        <span class="ug-chip" role="listitem">Source + docs</span><span class="ug-chip" role="listitem">Source only</span><span class="ug-chip" role="listitem">Docs only</span>
        <span class="ug-chip" role="listitem">Contracts/schema only</span><span class="ug-chip" role="listitem">Infrastructure only</span><span class="ug-chip" role="listitem">Supporting/shared source</span>
    </div>
    <p>A multi-package project is normal: for example application source in one package, authored documentation in another, and contracts or infrastructure in a third.</p>

    <h3 id="project-tab" class="ug-subtitle">Evidence roles on the Project tab</h3>
    <div class="ug-table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Project evidence roles" tabindex="0"><table class="ug-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Role</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Used by</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Application source</th><td>Required · one package</td><td>Project definition (main application)</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Project documentation</th><td>Required · several allowed</td><td>Explorers and document reviews</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Contracts and schemas</th><td>Optional</td><td>Schema Studio and contract-source discovery</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Infrastructure</th><td>Optional</td><td>Infrastructure and configuration reviews</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Supporting/shared source</th><td>Optional</td><td>Integration discovery and source reviews</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Generated documentation</th><td>Optional · one package</td><td>Generated Documentation Health and Documentation Drift</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <ul class="ug-list">
        <li><strong>1 · Target Application first:</strong> choose the application on the Target Application tab — detected applications, documentation projects and names already used by a Target Environment. Infrastructure, contract, shared-library and generated-docs packages are evidence, never an application. <em>+ Add application manually</em> names one before any evidence exists. Without a Target Application, the Project tab shows <em>Target Application required</em>. Changing the application archives the previous application's evidence (never deletes it); choosing it again restores it.</li>
        <li><strong>2 · Confirm its evidence:</strong> a checklist per role. <em>Strong match</em> (the project's own package, an existing binding, an import recorded under the project's name) is selected by default; <em>Possible match</em> (name similarity only) never is. Each suggestion says why. Deselect anything; a deselection is remembered and never silently undone. <em>+ Add imported package</em> adds another compatible import.</li>
        <li><strong>3 · Expected evidence:</strong> <em>+ Add expected evidence</em> (for example “SkoleAdapter source”) records a required role that stays Missing until a matching package is imported and bound — no package is created.</li>
        <li><strong>4 · Save Project:</strong> one save stores the project, its bindings and expected evidence. The <em>Advanced evidence bindings</em> table below uses the same bindings for per-role readiness, provenance (<em>Bound from Project setup</em>, <em>Bound manually</em>) and Change / add. Switching to another project asks first.</li>
        <li><strong>Test Pack evidence:</strong> an activated Test Pack can add required roles; they appear with the pack that needs them.</li>
        <li><strong>Remove safely:</strong> removing or changing a binding never deletes an imported package or a Source Analysis snapshot, and BirkNext never silently selects the latest import.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="project-readiness" class="ug-subtitle">Bound is not Ready</h3>
    <p>Readiness is role-specific. A bound package can lack the capability the role needs: application source may be <em>Ready</em> while Contracts/schema is <em>Partial</em>. The summary reads, for example, “3 / 3 bound · 2 ready”, and <em>Required evidence</em> is Ready only when every required role is Ready.</p>
    <div class="ug-status-row">
        <span class="status-chip status-chip-ready">Ready</span><span class="status-chip status-chip-partial">Partial</span><span class="status-chip status-chip-info">Suggested</span>
        <span class="status-chip status-chip-warning">Needs decision</span><span class="status-chip status-chip-critical">Missing</span><span class="status-chip status-chip-critical">Conflict · review required</span>
    </div>
    <GuideCallout Kind="CalloutKind.Tip"><p>Optional evidence never blocks. <em>Conflict</em> means a single-package role has more than one source bound; choose one.</p></GuideCallout>
</section>

<section id="source-analysis" class="ug-section ug-topic" data-topic="source-analysis" aria-labelledby="source-analysis-title">
    <h2 id="source-analysis-title" class="ug-section-title" tabindex="-1">Source Analysis</h2>
    <GuideArticleMeta Topic="source-analysis" />
    <p class="ug-lead"><a href="/source-analysis">Source Analysis</a> turns an archive into an immutable, fingerprinted snapshot that other features select by reference. It needs no Target Environment, and reviews never need a duplicate upload.</p>
    <p>Areas:</p><ul class="ug-chip-row ug-chip-list">@foreach (var domain in DocumentationCatalog.DocumentedSourceAnalysisDomains) { <li class="ug-chip">@domain</li> }</ul>
    <ul class="ug-list">
        <li>Import Project creates the snapshot automatically (<em>Created by: Project Import</em>); <em>Upload source only</em> remains for source-only archives.</li>
        <li>Re-analysis creates a new snapshot. Consumers keep the exact snapshot they used; a newer one may be offered, never silently switched.</li>
        <li>Source Analysis describes declarations and analyzer findings — not deployment state, runtime behavior or successful pipeline runs.</li>
        <li data-testid="guide-glossary-rows"><strong>Project glossary:</strong> only rows of the glossary's term tables are terms. Tables headed <em>Kind</em> (naming conventions), <em>Owner</em> (pinned names), <em>Issue</em> (known inconsistencies) and <em>Source</em> (the glossary's inputs) are kept as evidence under their own section and listed under <em>Sources &amp; authority</em>, never as terms. Proposal documents stay proposals. A snapshot analyzed before this rule says that a re-analysis separates them.</li>
    </ul>
    <ReviewDisclosure Text="Advanced details: archive handling" CollapseText="Hide advanced details" TestId="ug-source-advanced">
        <p>Safe ZIPs up to 50 MB compressed and 100 MB expanded, including nested folders, several services, document-only archives and unsupported technologies (reported as an analysis limitation). No traversal paths or symbolic links; archives are read in memory, never extracted to disk.</p>
    </ReviewDisclosure>
    <h3 id="source-management" class="ug-subtitle">Source Management: which version is analysed</h3>
    <p><a href="/sources">Source Management</a> lists every imported source with its capabilities (read from its content), its role, the application it belongs to and how far it is analysed. <em>Import source</em> and <em>Upload new version</em> add evidence; <em>Snapshot Registry</em> shows the stored snapshots and their history. Import Project brings evidence in; Source Management is where you inspect it.</p>
    <ul class="ug-list">
        <li><strong>Analysis status and review are different things.</strong> <em>Analyzed</em>, <em>Partly analyzed</em> and <em>Not analyzed</em> say how much of a source BirkNext could analyse (per area under <em>Analysis by area</em>). <em>Requires review</em> means a person must decide something, such as a role or an application association. Zero items requiring review does not mean the analysis is complete.</li>
        <li><strong>Analysis baseline.</strong> When one source has several imported versions (another ZIP, another branch), choose which one BirkNext analyses. The newest import never becomes the baseline by itself, and without a choice BirkNext draws no implementation-specific conclusion from that source. With one version, that version is used.</li>
        <li><strong>The analysis baseline is not the deployed version.</strong> It says what BirkNext reads, not what runs in an environment.</li>
        <li><strong>Identity.</strong> A ZIP without repository metadata is grouped by its archive name, and the page says so; that is an inferred identity, not verified Git provenance.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="technology-coverage" class="ug-subtitle">Technology Coverage</h3>
    <p><a href="/technology-coverage">Technology Coverage</a> shows detected technologies and what BirkNext supports per dimension — configuration, source analysis, contracts, runtime observation, active tests, test plans. Unsupported is an analyzer or provider limitation; it does not reduce project quality.</p>
</section>

<section id="explorers" class="ug-section ug-topic" data-topic="explorers" aria-labelledby="explorers-title">
    <h2 id="explorers-title" class="ug-section-title" tabindex="-1">Explorers and documents</h2>
    <GuideArticleMeta Topic="explorers" />
    <p class="ug-lead">The <a href="/specification-explorer">Specification Explorer</a>, <a href="/constitution-explorer">Constitution Explorer</a>, <a href="/plan-explorer">Plan Explorer</a>, <a href="/task-explorer">Task Explorer</a> and <a href="/data-model-explorer">Data Model Explorer</a> read the documents of the logical Project. Documentation may come from a different package than the application source.</p>
    <ul class="ug-list">
        <li><strong>Where documents come from:</strong> a Demo Project, else the documentation bound on the Project tab, else the current import; plus documents imported into the workspace with an Explorer’s <em>Import</em> action. Each artifact shows the package it came from.</li>
        <li><strong>Which document opens:</strong> the one you selected, else the only Approved/Baseline artifact, else the only artifact. Otherwise the Explorer asks you to choose; it never opens the first file found and never merges documents.</li>
        <li><strong>Three empty states:</strong> no project loaded (offers Import Project); several candidates (<em>needs selection</em>); no candidate (<em>not detected</em>, neutrally).</li>
        <li><strong>Identifiers:</strong> project-defined, such as JIRA-123 or US-A1 — no single ID scheme is assumed.</li>
        <li data-testid="guide-dme-efcore"><strong>Data Model Explorer and generated EF Core documentation:</strong> a section headed with a named EF Core context (<code>## Context: OrdersDbContext (schema orders)</code>) declares tables; each <code>### table (ClrType)</code> with a <em>Column | Type | Nullable | Constraints</em> table becomes a table with its declared types, nullability and keys. Only a standalone <code>UQ</code> marks a unique column (<code>UQ (a, b)</code> and <code>in UQ</code> are composite keys); <code>IX</code> declarations become indexes. A relationship-table row becomes a relationship only when From and To each name one table; rows marked <em>logical</em> are never called foreign keys, and list or prose rows stay unsupported.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3 id="specification-review" class="ug-subtitle">Specification Explorer counts entities</h3>
    <p><em>Requirements</em>, <em>Tests</em> and <em>Clarifications analyzed</em> count unique items (one FR-004, however often mentioned). <em>Review candidates</em> are suggestions for human review, not findings or defects. Traceability links can be many-to-many, and clarification links never count as test coverage. A requirement or a designed test parsed from the document is not, by itself, evidence of coverage.</p>
    <p>Browse the structure (search, <em>Expand all</em> / <em>Collapse all</em>), then select a user story, requirement or scenario: it opens as a full-width view below the structure with its complete text, Given/When/Then steps, success criteria, clarifications and links. <em>Back to structure</em> returns to the same row with the tree as you left it. <em>Specification links</em> lists each relationship type with its own count — a test <em>organized under a user story</em> is not a test <em>linked to a requirement ID</em>, and neither is reviewed coverage (see <a href="@UserGuideContent.Href("reviewed-coverage")">Reviewed requirement coverage</a>); the linked items open on request. <em>Source content not structured</em> lists text that was kept but not mapped to an Explorer field: information, not a parsing error. Preparing tests from scenarios happens in <a href="/test-plan-suggestions">Test Plan Suggestions</a>.</p>
    <h3 id="task-explorer" class="ug-subtitle">Task Explorer keeps five facts apart</h3>
    <ul class="ug-list">
        <li><em>Marked done in Task artifact</em> counts checked boxes — not implementation or test completion.</li>
        <li><em>Planning</em> counts parallelizable tasks — structure, not progress.</li>
        <li><em>Traceability</em> counts tasks with and without links; the two always add up to the total.</li>
        <li><em>Implementation evidence</em> and <em>Test evidence</em> come from Source Analysis and recorded tests; without them they read <em>Not assessed</em>, never zero.</li>
        <li><em>Task topics</em> (testing, security …) come from wording — a task that mentions testing is not a linked test asset.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Artifact governance covers revisions, fingerprints, authority, baselines and supersession; it is not a complete approval or sign-off system.</p>
</section>

<section id="generated-documentation" class="ug-section ug-topic" data-topic="generated-documentation" aria-labelledby="generated-documentation-title">
    <h2 id="generated-documentation-title" class="ug-section-title" tabindex="-1">Generated documentation</h2>
    <GuideArticleMeta Topic="generated-documentation" />
    <p class="ug-lead">Some projects keep documentation that a tool, build step or agent writes from the code (for example per-module <code>autodoc/</code> folders). BirkNext treats it as <strong>implementation-derived evidence</strong> — useful, but never automatically authoritative.</p>
    <div class="ug-card-grid ug-card-grid-2">
        <article class="ug-card"><h3><a href="/generated-documentation-explorer">Generated Documentation Explorer</a></h3><p>Browse the captured documents of the selected import by module and type — Data Model, architecture, data flow, messaging, overview. Read-only, with provenance.</p></article>
        <article class="ug-card"><h3><a href="/generated-documentation-health">Generated Documentation Health</a></h3><p>Compares structured keys (routes, messaging resources, entities, configuration keys, contract operations) with source, contracts and authored artifacts of the same import, per module and source snapshot.</p></article>
    </div>
    <ul class="ug-list">
        <li>Authored documentation ≠ generated documentation ≠ source evidence ≠ runtime evidence.</li>
        <li data-testid="guide-gde-read"><strong>Reading a document:</strong> select a document card and it opens in <em>Read</em> — the document itself, in source order. Mermaid sequence and flow diagrams are drawn from their own source (Diagram · Steps · Source, zoom and fit); a drawn diagram is documentation, not verified runtime architecture. Raw HTML in imported documents is shown as text and images from other hosts are not loaded. <em>Outline</em> lists the headings and jumps to them; <em>Source</em> is the original text with line numbers and copy; <em>Provenance</em> holds the document facts. A link with <code>?view=outline|source|provenance</code> opens that view.</li>
        <li data-testid="guide-gdh-focus"><strong>Health:</strong> the overall state explains itself (for example, which modules have partial generated output — having documents in every module is not completeness). Select a module name to open its details: why it needs review, its facts and a link to each of its generated documents in the Explorer. <em>Needs review</em> is one list; selecting an item opens the same details. Freshness <em>Not enough evidence</em> is never treated as stale.</li>
        <li>Every discrepancy is “Potential drift – requires review”. A generated document never supersedes the Specification and never verifies a completed task.</li>
        <li>Freshness is Current, Potentially stale or Unknown, judged per module from declared dates and dated source evidence. No discrepancy found does not mean fully synchronized.</li>
        <li>Without generated documentation these pages say so; it is optional evidence.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section id="traceability" class="ug-section ug-topic" data-topic="traceability" aria-labelledby="traceability-title">
    <h2 id="traceability-title" class="ug-section-title" tabindex="-1">Traceability</h2>
    <GuideArticleMeta Topic="traceability" />
    <p class="ug-lead"><a href="/artifact-traceability">Requirements Traceability</a> presents the shared graph: requirement → plan → task → implementation evidence → designed test → test execution. Links may be partial or stale, and the graph never turns source links into runtime verification.</p>
    <div class="ug-card-grid">
        <article class="ug-card" id="implementation-review"><h3><a href="/task-alignment">Task Alignment Review</a></h3><p>Checks every task against the Specification: <em>Direct spec link</em>, <em>Technical only</em>, <em>Needs review</em> or <em>Possible deviation</em> (only the last two are findings). It does not read source code, so it is not full implementation verification. Results are bound to both artifacts’ fingerprints (Current, Stale, Not run).</p></article>
        <article class="ug-card"><h3><a href="/implementation-review">Implementation Evidence Review</a></h3><p>Source-backed implementation and test evidence per requirement, once source is analyzed and the Artifact Pack is bound to source.</p></article>
        <article class="ug-card"><h3><a href="/implementation-traceability">Change Traceability</a></h3><p>Optional. Traces Azure DevOps work items through pull requests, commits and files. Without Azure DevOps it reads <em>Not configured</em>; change provenance never proves a requirement is implemented correctly.</p></article>
    </div>
    <p>Imported test results such as TRX are execution records. Source-discovered test definitions, planned tests, execution records, results and requirement links remain separate.</p>

    <h3 id="relationship-review" class="ug-subtitle">Relationship Review</h3>
    <p><a href="/correlation-review">Relationship Review</a> (Traceability &amp; impact) is where relationships between project evidence — requirements, user stories, source code, APIs, contracts and tests — are found and decided once, for every module that uses them.</p>
    <ul class="ug-list">
        <li><strong>Overview</strong>: run <em>analyze relationships</em> for the current workspace. Until then the page says <em>Not analyzed</em> and has nothing to decide.</li>
        <li><strong>Needs Review</strong>: <em>Required decisions</em> change or block an interpretation elsewhere; <em>Optional suggestions</em> count nowhere until someone confirms them. Confirm or reject each one; your decision names the evidence it was made on.</li>
        <li><strong>Relationships</strong> and <strong>History &amp; Governance</strong>: what is established, who decided and when. A decision made on older evidence becomes a suggestion again when that evidence changes.</li>
    </ul>
    <GuideCallout Kind="CalloutKind.Important"><p>A suggestion is never treated as established, and a confirmed relationship is not proof that anything was implemented, executed or passed.</p></GuideCallout>

    <h3 id="reviewed-coverage" class="ug-subtitle">Reviewed requirement coverage</h3>
    <p>Test Plan Suggestions, Project Quality and Recommended Workflow show how many requirements have <strong>reviewed scenario coverage</strong>. A requirement counts only when all of this holds:</p>
    <ol class="ug-list">
        <li>it is a requirement the specification itself defines (an id only seen in a test reference does not count);</li>
        <li>its link to a scenario is verified — named by the published system guide, or confirmed in Relationship Review on the current evidence;</li>
        <li>a tester accepted the <em>current</em> revision of that scenario for preparation in Test Plan Suggestions;</li>
        <li>the scenario has not changed since (a link proposed on older scenario text is not verified for the new one).</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Otherwise the requirement shows <em>Association verified · scenario not reviewed</em>, <em>Needs relationship review</em> or <em>No documented scenario coverage</em>. Similar wording never counts, and reviewed coverage is preparation, not execution: only Test Execution &amp; Evidence says a test ran.</p>
</section>
